// The Trending page's H1 (Mike, Nov 5): one sentence that tells the story of the What people are saying cards, 1.6 lines at most (about 130 characters), key takeaways highlighted. `marks` are exact phrases from `text`; tone w = worry, n2 = neutral, b = good; `say` is the SLACK index the phrase links to. Rewrite it on every refresh from the cards below.
const SLACKSTORY = {"text":"Redbubble's topics nav restarts as a clean test, its sticker cross-sell edges up, and Dashery admin data is now live.","marks":[{"t":"restarts as a clean test","tone":"w","say":1},{"t":"edges up","tone":"n2","say":2},{"t":"admin data is now live","tone":"b","say":0}]};
const SLACK = [
  {"icon":"📊","site":"Dashery","title":"Dashery admin events are now live in Amplitude, with a dashboard","when":"Nov 9","url":"https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0V4PM9DW/p1794243061498929","refs":[{"url":"https://app.amplitude.com/analytics/tpub/dashboard/6p6i0qsm","l":"Dashery admin eventing dashboard"}],"status":"Live Nov 9","tone":"done","channel":"#released","fx":"Not on the CVR pages: it is creator-side data, so it will not move TeePublic or Redbubble CVR. Dashery has no CVR page yet.","body":"The Creator Admin now sends first-party events to Amplitude: logo, header and favicon set, store color and font changes, pricing, earnings-report downloads, nav link clicks, design publishes and admin dashboard visits. The Dashery team wants to see which admin steps come before a creator's first sale, how many designs are uploaded before it, and whether creators fall into distinct login rhythms.","tldr":"Dashery admin actions are now measured, so the team can start asking what creators do before they sell.","pts":["Tracked now: logo, header, favicon, store color, font, pricing, earnings downloads, nav clicks, design publishes and admin dashboard visits.","A new Amplitude dashboard, Dashery admin eventing, is up.","Questions it unlocks: what comes before a first sale, designs uploaded before it, login cadence, how many creators change standard pricing."],"qs":["Which admin step is the strongest early sign that a creator will make a first sale?"],"why":"This is the data Dashery needs for its own measures; expect first reads once a few weeks of events have built up."},
  {"icon":"🧭","site":"RB","title":"Redbubble topics-first navigation test restarts as a clean two-variant run; nothing ships into peak","when":"Nov 9","url":"https://notes.granola.ai/d/7277db8e-577e-4040-abdc-b195ee1ebbe4","refs":[{"test":"1409398","l":"Topics-first navigation test"}],"status":"Restarting","tone":"warn","channel":"Customer working group","fx":"No: the test covers only the shoppers in it, and Redbubble CVR on the CVR pages is not affected by the variants.","body":"The Customer working group reviewed the test from Oct 7 to Nov 6 with bots filtered out. Navigation engagement was up 6.6% to 8.0% across the variants (mobile up about a third, desktop down about 22%), but sitewide CVR was lower in all three (-0.97%, -0.10% and -1.03%) and revenue per user was down 0.5% to 1.9%. Bot traffic labeled as desktop polluted the device splits, though bots were spread evenly across the test groups. The group decided to restart with only control against the T-shirt-default version, judge the whole test rather than by device, and not ship any variant before peak.","tldr":"High engagement without a conversion gain, so the test restarts clean and nothing ships before peak.","pts":["Engagement up 6.6% to 8.0%, but sitewide CVR down in all three variants and revenue per user down 0.5% to 1.9%.","Device splits are not reliable because bots were counted as desktop.","Restart: control against T-shirt-default only, read as one A against B.","No variant ships in peak; rerun in the new year if needed."],"qs":["Does the T-shirt-default version hold flat CVR once bot traffic is removed from the start?"],"why":"Read the restarted test on the whole-site result, not on engagement alone."},
  {"icon":"🛒","site":"RB","title":"Redbubble sticker cross-sell after about 10 days: both versions up on revenue per user, not yet significant","when":"Nov 9","url":"https://notes.granola.ai/d/7277db8e-577e-4040-abdc-b195ee1ebbe4","refs":[{"test":"1580138","l":"Cart cross-sell test"},{"soon":"customer","l":"Customer priorities"}],"status":"Running","tone":"open","channel":"Customer working group","fx":"Not in the CVR pages yet: it is a running test with about 60% confidence, so it reads as a direction only.","body":"The cart test has run about 10 days with two versions. With the full trust badges, CVR is flat to +0.5% and revenue per user is up about 1%; with trimmed badges, CVR is -0.6% and revenue per user is up about 1.5%. Both are net positive on revenue per user but small and noisy. The team will leave it running while cart conversion holds, wait on a gross-profit-per-order read, and consider free shipping with the cross-sell item in the new year. Quick-add on the cart tray moves into next sprint.","tldr":"Both cross-sell versions lift revenue per user a little, at about 60% confidence, so the test keeps running.","pts":["Full badges: CVR flat to +0.5%, revenue per user about +1%.","Trimmed badges: CVR -0.6%, revenue per user about +1.5%.","Significance is about 60%, so still noisy.","Next: a gross profit per order read, then quick-add on the cart tray next sprint."],"qs":["Does gross profit per order stay positive once the sticker margin is counted?"],"why":"A revenue-per-user gain with lower CVR needs the gross profit read before it can be called a win."},
  {"icon":"☀️","site":"Both","title":"Redbubble closed Sunday Nov 8 at about $472K, its best day of the week, with the 48-hour timer live; TeePublic revenue slipped","when":"Nov 8","url":"https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89","refs":[{"go":"rb|overall","l":"Redbubble CVR"},{"go":"tp|overall","l":"TeePublic CVR"}],"status":"Watching","tone":"open","channel":"#peak_daily_performance_standup","fx":"Yes, partly: Nov 8 is now in the numbers. Redbubble revenue was about $472.2K on 11,297 orders (up from $418.7K on Nov 7) and its bot-filtered CVR 1.70% at Strong. TeePublic was about $437.4K on 9,356 orders (down from $475.2K on Nov 7) and 2.68% at Strong, a lower Sunday, as the marketing team saw. The Nov 7 and Nov 8 gain also carries the 48-hour timer, the sale and new feed products together, so the timer alone is not isolated.","body":"On Sunday morning the marketing team said Redbubble revenue per marketing dollar looked almost too good to believe, with spend up only about 10% on the day, and that it expected to give some back to budget in the USA, the weakest region. On TeePublic, Shopping spend ran a little hot with no Sunday morning revenue lift. A product leader asked whether a Redbubble timer difference was behind it, since running the timer for the whole promotion rather than the last 24 hours had won a test. On Monday morning the marketing team added that in the timer test the last 48 hours and last 72 hours performed about the same, with 48 slightly ahead, and that this weekend was the first time the extended timer showed on Redbubble (Saturday and Sunday, US time).","tldr":"Redbubble had its best day of the week on Nov 8, its first weekend with the 48-hour timer, while TeePublic revenue slipped.","pts":["Redbubble Nov 8: about $472.2K on 11,297 orders, against $418.7K on 10,131 orders on Nov 7.","TeePublic Nov 8: about $437.4K on 9,356 orders, against $475.2K on 9,975 orders on Nov 7.","Marketing saw Redbubble revenue per marketing dollar well above range, with spend up about 10% day over day.","Timer test: 48 and 72 hours performed similarly, 48 slightly ahead; this weekend was its first run on Redbubble.","TeePublic Shopping spend ran a little hot on Sunday morning with no revenue lift."],"qs":["Was the Redbubble gain the timer, the sale ending, or the new feed products, and will it hold once the promotion is over?"],"why":"Read Nov 8 on the CVR pages against the Redbubble timer start before calling it a site change."},
  {"icon":"🧭","site":"TP","title":"TeePublic is shipping the T-shirt-default topics nav: no CVR change, engagement up 7.6%","when":"Nov 7","url":"https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0CF5E7KVPX/p1793983863807579","refs":[{"go":"tp|overall","l":"TeePublic CVR"}],"status":"Shipping","tone":"done","channel":"#temp-tpo-localized-search-and-nav","fx":"No: nothing about the nav is in the numbers shown yet. The test read no meaningful change in CVR and +7.6% nav engagement at significance; only about 1% of visitors use the nav, so sitewide CVR will not show it either way.","body":"The team decided on Nov 7 to ship the topics nav. The test sat at almost no impact on CVR (not statistically significant), while engagement rose 7.6% at significance, and engagement is the lever for the longer-term plan to drive search and personalization through search engagement. The separate Trending nav test (trending topics by location) still needs agreed measures and guardrails before it launches: nav engagement, trending against other topics at the same position, downstream exploration, and ATC, CVR and revenue per visitor as guardrails.","tldr":"The topics nav ships after a flat CVR read and +7.6% engagement; the Trending nav test still needs agreed measures.","pts":["Topics nav test: CVR flat and not significant, nav engagement +7.6% at significance.","Decision on Nov 7: ship it, using engagement to drive search and personalization.","Trending nav test (by location) still needs measures and guardrails before launch.","About 1% of visitors use the nav, so sitewide CVR will not show a nav-only change."],"qs":["Which measures and guardrails does the Customer team agree to for the Trending nav test?"],"why":"Judge the nav on engagement and downstream clicks; do not expect sitewide CVR to move."}
];

